  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\nVisible satellite at 11 AM was showing clear skies across Southern \nCalifornia. Highs today are expected to be in the upper 70s to upper \n80s for most locations, with spots in the mountains in the 50 to 60 \ndegree range. The pressure gradient between San Diego and Tonopah, \nNV was still weakly offshore this morning. Onshore flow is expected \nto return Monday as an upper level low pressure system moves into\nthe Pacific Northwest. Marine layer low clouds have a small \nchance of returning to some coastal locations Monday morning, with\nhigher confidence for low clouds returning by Tuesday morning. \nOnce marine layer low clouds and areas of fog return, they will be\npresent each night and morning through the week. \n\nHigh temperatures Monday will be similar to today's. High \ntemperatures in coastal areas will be 5 to 10 degrees above average, \nwith temperatures closer to average further inland. By Wednesday, \nforecast has widespread below average temperatures. Temperatures are \nforecast to remain below average through the remainder of the \nweek.\n\nGlobal ensembles continue to indicate unsettled weather for \nthe end of the week into the first days of November. There \nremains a lot of uncertainty in the details, which will ultimately\nbe dictated by how far south the upper level low off the coast of\nthe Pacific Northwest tracks. Rain chances are in the forecast as\nearly as Friday. In addition to precipitation, cooler, cloudier, \nand windier conditions are expected.",
